    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Problem\n\n[#40475](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/40475) improved the error message when *indexing* `vim.NIL`, but it does not cover `vim.NIL` being passed to `ipairs`, `pairs`, `next`, or `#`. These still fail with:\n\n```\nbad argument #1 to '?' (table expected, got userdata)\n```\n\nThis is exactly the failure mode from [#40390](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/40390): an LSP server sends `null` for an optional field (e.g. `Diagnostic.tags`), `vim.json.decode` maps it to `vim.NIL` (which is truthy), so `field or {}` passes it through and the subsequent `ipairs(...)` crashes deep inside the LSP code with no hint of the offending field.\n\n[#40422](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/40422) addressed this but was closed with the assumption it was made unnecessary by #40475. It wasn't — see [this comment](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/40422#issuecomment-4902504107) for failing tests demonstrating this.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n```lua\nlocal parsed = vim.json.decode('{\"foo\": null}')\nfor _, v in ipairs(parsed.foo) do end\n-- E5108: ... bad argument #1 to 'ipairs' (table expected, got userdata)\n```\n\nSame for `pairs(parsed.foo)` and `next(parsed.foo)`.\n\nReal-world trigger: any LSP server sending `\"tags\": null` or `\"relatedInformation\": null` in diagnostics (#40390).\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nA clear error naming `vim.NIL` (analogous to #40475's \"attempt to index vim.NIL\"), e.g. \"attempt to iterate vim.NIL\".\n\n### Related\n\n- Original issue: [#40390](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/40390)\n- Closed PR: [#40422](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/40422) (closed as presumed fixed by #40475)\n- Partial fix: [#40475](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/40475) (indexing only)\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nv0.13.0-dev-806+g19f64a2680\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nN/A — Vim has no built-in Lua support.\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nLinux (Arch), kernel 7.0.3-arch1-1\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nwezterm\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nnightly binary",
